WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.360
Today we're learning one of the most useful ideas

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:05.080
in difficult maths puzzles: an invariant.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:08.200
That's the name for a quantity that stays fixed even

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:09.220
when other things change.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:11.020
Sometimes it's a total.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:12.660
Sometimes it's a difference.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:15.260
And sometimes it's something less obvious.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:17.000
Why does that matter?

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.040
Imagine a game with so many legal moves that you

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.380
could spend all afternoon trying different routes.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:24.580
You don't need to test every path.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:28.280
If every move preserves one mathematical fact, that

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:30.300
fact can tell you when a target is impossible.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:32.140
Here's what we'll do.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.200
We'll begin with a puzzle, watch one legal move,

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.000
and discover what stays the same.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.180
We'll then change the rules, work through two examples,

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:42.680
and prove why our shortcuts really work.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:46.020
Finally, you'll try an invariant question for yourself.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:49.840
Let's begin. One pile has five gold counters.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:52.040
The other has seven blue counters.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:55.440
You may transfer counters between the piles, as often

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:56.000
as you like.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.940
Could you ever finish with ten counters in each pile?

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.260
Before you try dozens of moves, ask a better question.

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:05.380
Is there something that no legal move can change?

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:09.220
Watch closely. Move two blue counters to the gold pile.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:12.100
Now there are seven on the left and five on

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.900
the right. The picture has changed, but the total

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:16.259
is still twelve.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:19.180
Every transfer takes some counters away from one pile

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:21.580
and adds exactly that many to the other.

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:23.000
Nothing is created.

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:24.300
Nothing disappears.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:26.900
So ten and ten is impossible.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:30.120
That would require twenty counters, and we have only

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:32.760
twelve. Six and six, however, is possible.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:36.780
A quantity that remains unchanged after every permitted

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:38.340
move is called an invariant.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:41.680
Here, the invariant is the total number of counters.

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:43.420
But here's the twist.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:44.700
Change the rule.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.820
Each move now adds two brand-new counters to both

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.478
piles. What stays fixed this time?

00:01:50.808 --> 00:01:53.646
The total is growing, by four with every move.

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:56.180
So the total is no longer an invariant.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:58.660
Look at the difference between the two piles instead.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:02.280
We begin with five and seven: a gap of two.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.750
Add two to each and we get seven and nine.

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:07.470
Add two again, and we get nine and eleven.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.287
The gap is always two.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:11.940
Let's prove this, rather than just spot a pattern.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:14.300
Call the two pile sizes A and B.

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:17.060
After a move, their sizes are A plus two

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:18.169
and B plus two.

00:02:18.419 --> 00:02:20.265
Now subtract the new sizes.

00:02:20.265 --> 00:02:23.589
The positive two and negative two cancel perfectly.

00:02:23.589 --> 00:02:25.380
We are left with A minus B.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:27.100
So the difference cannot change.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:29.320
Go back to transferring counters.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:32.660
If the first pile loses t and the second gains

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:36.040
t, the total becomes A minus t, plus B plus

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:38.400
t. Again, the changes cancel.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:40.480
Here's the important trap.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:43.960
Moving counters preserves the total, but not usually

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:46.600
the difference. Adding the same number to both

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:49.160
preserves their difference, but changes the total.

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:50.720
Try one yourself.

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:53.880
Start with four counters in one pile and nine

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:54.420
in the other.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:56.820
Each move adds two counters to both.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:02:59.265
Could you reach eighteen and twenty-three?

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:02.640
Yes. The difference starts at five and ends at five.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:06.000
Exactly seven moves add fourteen counters to each pile.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:07.820
Both target numbers work.

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:10.350
But could you reach eighteen and twenty-two?

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:13.440
No. Their difference is four, not five.

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:15.440
The invariant rules that out instantly.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:18.220
This is the power of an invariant.

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:20.300
You don't need to simulate a hundred moves.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:24.660
Examine one move, identify what cancels, and use that

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:26.140
to reason about every move.

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:29.940
Pause here. Draw one move in your notebook, and write

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:32.340
a single equation showing what remains unchanged.

